1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,532
You smash the ball as hard as you can.

2
00:00:02,882 --> 00:00:06,378
It comes straight back at your feet,
and you lose the point.

3
00:00:06,378 --> 00:00:06,722
Again.

4
00:00:07,322 --> 00:00:11,565
To climb the pickleball ladder, hitting
harder isn't the main thing.

5
00:00:11,565 --> 00:00:12,549
Missing less is.

6
00:00:13,349 --> 00:00:18,234
Today: the five levels, which one you're really
at, and the skill that moves you up.

7
00:00:18,584 --> 00:00:22,481
USA Pickleball's skill levels run from 1.

8
00:00:22,481 --> 00:00:23,501
0 up to 5.

9
00:00:23,501 --> 00:00:26,563
5-plus, mostly in half-point steps.

10
00:00:26,913 --> 00:00:30,136
Its own overview groups them
into five tiers.

11
00:00:30,136 --> 00:00:36,022
My five levels are cut slightly differently, one
skill each, and the names are mine.

12
00:00:36,372 --> 00:00:38,063
Level 1, the Starter.

13
00:00:38,063 --> 00:00:39,754
Level 2, the Rallier.

14
00:00:39,754 --> 00:00:41,445
Level 3, the Dropper.

15
00:00:41,445 --> 00:00:43,213
Level 4, the Resetter.

16
00:00:43,213 --> 00:00:45,289
And Level 5, No Weak Spots.

17
00:00:45,639 --> 00:00:47,827
And there's a hidden thread.

18
00:00:47,827 --> 00:00:48,280
At 3.

19
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:52,127
0, the official checklist asks
you to land serves,

20
00:00:52,127 --> 00:00:54,919
returns and volleys 7 times out of 10.

21
00:00:55,269 --> 00:00:56,085
At 3.

22
00:00:56,085 --> 00:00:57,582
5, it's 8.

23
00:00:57,582 --> 00:00:58,399
At 4.

24
00:00:58,399 --> 00:00:59,896
0, it's 9.

25
00:00:59,896 --> 00:01:00,712
At 4.

26
00:01:00,712 --> 00:01:01,937
5 and 5.

27
00:01:01,937 --> 00:01:04,250
0, it's 10 out of 10.

28
00:01:04,601 --> 00:01:08,868
So: new skills at every level, and
fewer misses on all of them.

29
00:01:09,567 --> 00:01:11,302
A DUPR rating is different.

30
00:01:11,302 --> 00:01:14,772
It comes from your match results,
not a skill checklist.

31
00:01:15,123 --> 00:01:20,605
When I quote DUPR numbers, they only cover
players with an active doubles rating,

32
00:01:20,605 --> 00:01:22,142
not everyone who plays.

33
00:01:22,491 --> 00:01:23,687
The official 2.

34
00:01:23,687 --> 00:01:29,296
5 player can keep a short rally going and
has a basic ability to keep score.

35
00:01:29,647 --> 00:01:33,117
It's like learning to drive in
an empty car park.

36
00:01:33,117 --> 00:01:34,851
Rules of the road first.

37
00:01:34,851 --> 00:01:35,684
Speed later.

38
00:01:36,034 --> 00:01:38,323
Rule one is the two-bounce rule.

39
00:01:38,323 --> 00:01:41,931
The serve must bounce, then the
return must bounce.

40
00:01:41,931 --> 00:01:44,290
Only after that can anyone volley.

41
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:49,129
Rule two is the kitchen: the zone 7 feet
from the net on each side.

42
00:01:49,129 --> 00:01:51,637
You can't volley while standing in it.

43
00:01:51,987 --> 00:01:56,460
The classic Starter moment: you serve,
rush the net, volley the return,

44
00:01:56,460 --> 00:01:57,640
and lose the point.

45
00:01:57,991 --> 00:02:03,547
Among players with a DUPR rating,
about 31% are below 3.

46
00:02:03,547 --> 00:02:03,644
0.

47
00:02:03,994 --> 00:02:09,397
Before any drill in this video, warm up for
5 to 8 minutes: light jogging,

48
00:02:09,397 --> 00:02:13,935
side shuffles, leg swings, walking
lunges and easy calf raises.

49
00:02:14,285 --> 00:02:17,016
Then soft dinks, building up to drives.

50
00:02:17,016 --> 00:02:21,453
Doctors who study pickleball injuries
recommend a proper warm-up.

51
00:02:21,803 --> 00:02:24,090
To move up, get the ball in play.

52
00:02:24,090 --> 00:02:26,309
Drill 1: the 10-ball serve test.

53
00:02:26,309 --> 00:02:29,739
Serve 10 from each side and
count how many land in.

54
00:02:30,089 --> 00:02:32,620
3 rounds, 2 or 3 times a week.

55
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:34,825
Your target is 7 out of 10.

56
00:02:35,175 --> 00:02:36,924
Drill 2: the dink count.

57
00:02:36,924 --> 00:02:42,589
With a partner at the kitchen line, trade soft
shots that bounce in the kitchen.

58
00:02:42,589 --> 00:02:44,198
3 rounds of 2 minutes.

59
00:02:44,198 --> 00:02:46,226
Aim for 10 in a row, then 20.

60
00:02:46,576 --> 00:02:49,374
New to sport, or coming back
after a break?

61
00:02:49,374 --> 00:02:53,445
Build your court time up gradually,
and don't play through pain.

62
00:02:53,795 --> 00:02:54,618
The mistake?

63
00:02:54,618 --> 00:02:56,453
Swinging hard to win points.

64
00:02:56,453 --> 00:02:59,427
At this level, the game is
keeping the ball in.

65
00:02:59,777 --> 00:03:01,293
Level 2 is the 3.

66
00:03:01,293 --> 00:03:07,948
0 player: medium-paced forehands, a basic dink
rally, and a backhand they avoid.

67
00:03:08,298 --> 00:03:11,525
Among DUPR-rated players, 3.

68
00:03:11,525 --> 00:03:12,416
0 to 3.

69
00:03:12,416 --> 00:03:17,535
49 is the single biggest
rating band: about 29%.

70
00:03:17,885 --> 00:03:21,026
The Rallier still makes
frequent unforced errors.

71
00:03:21,026 --> 00:03:23,538
Those are misses nobody forced you into.

72
00:03:23,889 --> 00:03:25,202
And here's the trap.

73
00:03:25,202 --> 00:03:29,953
The Rallier hits a medium-paced third shot
from the back with no real plan.

74
00:03:29,953 --> 00:03:34,641
The opponents are already at the net, and
they volley it back at your feet.

75
00:03:34,991 --> 00:03:36,590
Think of playing catch.

76
00:03:36,590 --> 00:03:40,388
Your job is to keep the ball going,
not to win the throw.

77
00:03:40,738 --> 00:03:43,591
To move up, return deep and get forward.

78
00:03:43,591 --> 00:03:44,078
The 3.

79
00:03:44,078 --> 00:03:49,924
5 checklist asks you to place serves and returns
deep, land them 8 times out of 10,

80
00:03:49,924 --> 00:03:53,751
and move up to the kitchen line
when you get the chance.

81
00:03:54,101 --> 00:03:55,995
The drill: return and run.

82
00:03:55,995 --> 00:03:57,468
Your partner serves.

83
00:03:57,468 --> 00:04:02,938
You return deep, then move up to the kitchen
line before the ball comes back.

84
00:04:02,938 --> 00:04:03,850
3 sets of 10.

85
00:04:04,201 --> 00:04:06,069
Move forward under control.

86
00:04:06,069 --> 00:04:07,670
Don't sprint and lunge.

87
00:04:07,670 --> 00:04:13,275
In one clinic's records, running or lunging
forward was the most common way players

88
00:04:13,275 --> 00:04:14,676
hurt a foot or ankle.

89
00:04:15,025 --> 00:04:15,857
The mistake?

90
00:04:15,857 --> 00:04:17,585
Hiding from your backhand.

91
00:04:17,585 --> 00:04:19,121
Train it now, not later.

92
00:04:19,471 --> 00:04:20,708
Level 3, the 3.

93
00:04:20,708 --> 00:04:26,660
5 player, is building the most famous shot in
pickleball: the third-shot drop.

94
00:04:27,011 --> 00:04:27,920
Why that shot?

95
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:33,012
Because of the two-bounce rule, the serving team
hits the third shot from the back,

96
00:04:33,012 --> 00:04:35,800
while the returners can
already be at the net.

97
00:04:36,150 --> 00:04:40,832
A drop is a soft shot that floats over the
net and lands in the kitchen.

98
00:04:40,832 --> 00:04:46,219
It's hard for the players at the net to attack,
so it buys you time to move forward.

99
00:04:46,569 --> 00:04:50,591
Think of a parachute: while the ball
floats down, you walk in.

100
00:04:50,591 --> 00:04:52,251
A hard drive is a rocket.

101
00:04:52,251 --> 00:04:54,804
Fast, but it can come right back at you.

102
00:04:55,154 --> 00:05:00,163
About 24% of DUPR players are rated 3.

103
00:05:00,163 --> 00:05:01,191
5 to 3.

104
00:05:01,191 --> 00:05:01,319
99.

105
00:05:01,669 --> 00:05:03,460
Drill 1: the drop ladder.

106
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:06,561
Start at the kitchen line
with a dink rally.

107
00:05:06,561 --> 00:05:11,177
Then step back 2 steps and keep landing
soft shots in the kitchen.

108
00:05:11,177 --> 00:05:15,173
When 4 land, step back again, all
the way to the baseline.

109
00:05:15,523 --> 00:05:18,101
Drill 2: third shot must be a drop.

110
00:05:18,101 --> 00:05:22,327
Play a game to 11 where every third
shot has to be a drop,

111
00:05:22,327 --> 00:05:24,547
even if it loses you the point.

112
00:05:24,897 --> 00:05:25,759
The mistake?

113
00:05:25,759 --> 00:05:29,737
Hitting a beautiful drop and
admiring it from the baseline.

114
00:05:29,737 --> 00:05:32,257
The drop exists to get you to the net.

115
00:05:32,607 --> 00:05:34,326
Level 4 covers 4.

116
00:05:34,326 --> 00:05:35,186
0 and 4.

117
00:05:35,186 --> 00:05:35,472
5.

118
00:05:35,472 --> 00:05:38,815
These players are learning the reset.

119
00:05:39,165 --> 00:05:40,863
A reset is a soft block.

120
00:05:40,863 --> 00:05:45,277
It takes the pace off a hard ball and
drops it into the kitchen.

121
00:05:45,277 --> 00:05:48,061
The rally goes from frantic back to calm.

122
00:05:48,411 --> 00:05:49,976
Picture catching an egg.

123
00:05:49,976 --> 00:05:52,166
Soft hands that give with the ball.

124
00:05:52,516 --> 00:05:54,004
Why does this matter?

125
00:05:54,004 --> 00:05:57,047
In one study of 8 pro men's
doubles matches,

126
00:05:57,047 --> 00:06:02,862
about 7 in 10 shots were played from what the
researchers called the transition zone,

127
00:06:02,862 --> 00:06:04,079
roughly mid-court.

128
00:06:04,429 --> 00:06:04,970
The 4.

129
00:06:04,970 --> 00:06:09,991
0 checklist asks you to block hard
volleys, start making resets,

130
00:06:09,991 --> 00:06:12,849
and know which balls you can attack.

131
00:06:12,849 --> 00:06:16,866
And serves, returns and volleys
now need 9 out of 10.

132
00:06:17,216 --> 00:06:22,191
About 15% of DUPR players are rated 4.

133
00:06:22,191 --> 00:06:23,211
0 to 4.

134
00:06:23,211 --> 00:06:23,339
99.

135
00:06:23,689 --> 00:06:25,865
The drill: the mid-court reset.

136
00:06:25,865 --> 00:06:27,021
Stand mid-court.

137
00:06:27,021 --> 00:06:31,917
Your partner at the kitchen line feeds
balls at a pace you can control,

138
00:06:31,917 --> 00:06:33,481
aimed below your waist.

139
00:06:33,831 --> 00:06:36,425
Block them softly into the kitchen.

140
00:06:36,425 --> 00:06:39,092
3 sets of 10, forehand and backhand.

141
00:06:39,092 --> 00:06:42,407
Only speed up once you're
landing 9 out of 10.

142
00:06:42,757 --> 00:06:46,604
This drill gets faster and brings
the ball near your face,

143
00:06:46,604 --> 00:06:49,669
so wear eye protection rated
for racket sports.

144
00:06:50,019 --> 00:06:50,828
The mistake?

145
00:06:50,828 --> 00:06:52,632
Swinging at every hard ball.

146
00:06:52,632 --> 00:06:56,675
At this level, you block it, drop it,
and wait for a better ball.

147
00:06:57,025 --> 00:06:58,150
Level 5 is 5.

148
00:06:58,150 --> 00:06:59,195
0 and above.

149
00:06:59,195 --> 00:07:04,901
These players have mastered the dink and
the drop, change pace easily,

150
00:07:04,901 --> 00:07:07,393
and rarely make unforced errors.

151
00:07:07,743 --> 00:07:11,502
Every shot works from both the
forehand and the backhand.

152
00:07:11,502 --> 00:07:13,901
Think of a castle with no weak wall.

153
00:07:13,901 --> 00:07:16,234
Whichever side you attack, it holds.

154
00:07:16,583 --> 00:07:20,728
Only about 1% of DUPR players are rated 5.

155
00:07:20,728 --> 00:07:21,788
0 or higher.

156
00:07:22,139 --> 00:07:23,952
Drill 1: weak-side week.

157
00:07:23,952 --> 00:07:29,392
10 minutes per session of backhand-only
dinks and drops, 3 sessions a week.

158
00:07:29,742 --> 00:07:31,454
Drill 2: the error tally.

159
00:07:31,454 --> 00:07:36,724
Ask a friend to make a tick mark for every
unforced error you make in one game,

160
00:07:36,724 --> 00:07:39,160
just like the official rating sheet.

161
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:41,795
Then train the shot with the most ticks.

162
00:07:42,145 --> 00:07:46,162
Strength train all your major muscle
groups at least 2 days a week,

163
00:07:46,162 --> 00:07:48,289
and start light if you're new to it.

164
00:07:48,639 --> 00:07:53,552
If something already hurts, like your
calf, Achilles, knee or shoulder,

165
00:07:53,552 --> 00:07:56,895
see a physio or sports doctor
before adding load.

166
00:07:57,245 --> 00:08:03,101
In a review of sports trials, strength training
cut injuries to about a third of the

167
00:08:03,101 --> 00:08:03,927
usual rate.

168
00:08:03,927 --> 00:08:08,061
That came from only a handful of
trials, none in pickleball.

169
00:08:08,411 --> 00:08:09,349
The mistake?

170
00:08:09,349 --> 00:08:10,432
Chasing power.

171
00:08:10,432 --> 00:08:13,969
The top level is defined by
control and variety.

172
00:08:13,969 --> 00:08:16,134
Pace is just one of the tools.

173
00:08:16,484 --> 00:08:22,108
In US emergency-room data, the most common way
people got hurt playing pickleball

174
00:08:22,108 --> 00:08:25,743
was falling, and most of those
patients were over 60.

175
00:08:26,093 --> 00:08:29,390
So when a lob sails over your
head, turn and run.

176
00:08:29,390 --> 00:08:30,445
Don't backpedal.

177
00:08:30,795 --> 00:08:33,801
For players aged 18 to 34 in that data,

178
00:08:33,801 --> 00:08:38,534
the most common cause of injury was a
twist or a rolled ankle.

179
00:08:38,534 --> 00:08:42,742
In sports trials, balance training
lowered injury rates.

180
00:08:43,091 --> 00:08:47,420
A ball strike is the most common cause
of pickleball eye injuries,

181
00:08:47,420 --> 00:08:52,072
so eye protection rated for racket sports
is worth wearing at any level.

182
00:08:52,423 --> 00:08:57,544
And stop and get checked promptly if you feel a
sudden kick or snap at the back of

183
00:08:57,544 --> 00:09:00,444
your heel or calf, or can't
push off your toes.

184
00:09:00,794 --> 00:09:05,663
Same if a hit to the eye leaves you with
blurred vision, flashes or pain,

185
00:09:05,663 --> 00:09:10,400
if a fall leaves your wrist swollen or
misshapen, if you hit your head,

186
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:13,295
or if a rolled ankle won't
take your weight.

187
00:09:13,645 --> 00:09:15,220
So, which level are you?

188
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:16,354
Count your misses.

189
00:09:16,705 --> 00:09:18,956
Can't land 7 serves out of 10?

190
00:09:18,956 --> 00:09:21,426
You're probably still on Level 1.

191
00:09:21,426 --> 00:09:23,895
Landing 7, but stuck at the back?

192
00:09:23,895 --> 00:09:26,582
Level 2: return deep and get forward.

193
00:09:26,932 --> 00:09:29,479
Getting forward, but always rushed?

194
00:09:29,479 --> 00:09:30,824
Level 3: the drop.

195
00:09:30,824 --> 00:09:32,735
Caught in mid-court a lot?

196
00:09:32,735 --> 00:09:34,079
Level 4: the reset.

197
00:09:34,429 --> 00:09:37,611
It's a rough self-check, not
an official rating.

198
00:09:37,611 --> 00:09:40,338
For that, a rater watches your whole game.

199
00:09:40,688 --> 00:09:42,679
This week, play 2 sessions.

200
00:09:42,679 --> 00:09:47,941
Warm up, do one 10-ball test, then spend 15
minutes on your level's drill.

201
00:09:48,291 --> 00:09:51,959
Remember that ball you smashed
straight back at your feet?

202
00:09:51,959 --> 00:09:54,819
Next time, hit it soft, and
walk in behind it.
